<article class="env-corollary" id="0BCF82">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0BCF82">Corollary <span data-tag="0BCF82">2.3.4.10</span>.</a>$n$-categories!and overcategories Let $\calC $ be an $n$-category and let $p: K \rightarrow \calC $ be a diagram. Then $\calC _{/p}$ is an $n$-category. </p>
</article>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      If $n \leq 0$, this follows easily from Examples <a href="/tag/B9E930" data-tag="B9E930">2.3.4.2</a> and <a href="/tag/E5AD33" data-tag="E5AD33">2.3.4.3</a>. We may therefore suppose that $n \geq 1$. Proposition <a href="/tag/A7327D" data-tag="A7327D">1.2.9.3</a> implies that $\calC _{/p}$ is an $\infty $-category. According to Proposition <a href="/tag/B877BB" data-tag="B877BB">2.3.4.9</a>, it suffices to show that for every $m > n$, $0 < i < m$, and every map $f_0: \Lambda ^ m_ i \rightarrow \calC _{/p}$, there exists a <em>unique</em> map $f: \Delta ^ m \rightarrow \calC _{/p}$ extending $f$. Equivalently, we must show that there is a unique map $g$ rendering the diagram 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix { \Lambda ^ m_ i \star K \ar @{^{(}->}[d] \ar [r]^{g_0} &amp;  \calC \\ \Delta ^ m \star K \ar @{-->}[ur]^{g} &amp;  } \]
</div>
    <p>
       commutative. The existence of $g$ follows from the fact that $\calC _{/p}$ is an $\infty $-category. Suppose that $g': \Delta ^ m \star K \rightarrow \calC $ is another map which extends $g_0$. Proposition <a href="/tag/B1B3FB" data-tag="B1B3FB">1.1.2.2</a> implies that $g' | \Delta ^ m = g | \Delta ^ m$. We conclude that $g$ and $g'$ coincide on the $n$-skeleton of $\Delta ^ m \star K$. Since $\calC $ is an $n$-category, we deduce that $g = g'$ as desired. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>